#: source/store/store-path.md:1
msgid "Store Path and Store Directory"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:3
msgid ""
"Nix's [store object](./store-object.md) and [file system object](./file-"
"system-object.md) data models are minimal and abstract. But to actually be "
"used by software, store objects need to be made available through the "
"operating system's file system."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:6
msgid ""
"This is done by exposing all the store objects in a single _[store directory]"
"(#store-directory-path)_. Every entry in that directory is a _[store path "
"base name](#base-name)_ pointing to a store object. Store objects exposed in "
"this way can then be referenced by _[store paths](#store-path)_."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:16
msgid "Store Path Base Name"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:18
msgid "Nix implements references to store objects as _store path base names_."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:20
msgid ""
"Think of a store path base name as an [opaque](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/"
"wiki/Opaque_data_type), [unique identifier](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Unique_identifier): The only way to obtain a store path base name is by "
"adding or building store objects. A store path base name will always "
"reference exactly one store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:27
msgid "Store path base names are pairs of"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:29
msgid "A 20-byte \\[digest\\]{#digest} for identification"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:30
msgid "A symbolic \\[name\\]{#name} for people to read"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:32 source/store/store-path.md:43
#: source/store/store-path.md:78
msgid "**Example**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:34
msgid "Digest: `q06x3jll2yfzckz2bzqak089p43ixkkq`"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:35
msgid "Name:   `firefox-33.1`"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:37
msgid "A store path base name is rendered to a string as the concatenation of"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:39
msgid ""
"Digest rendered in [Nix32](../protocols/nix32.md), a variant of [Base32]"
"(https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Base32) (20 hash bytes become 32 ASCII "
"characters)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:40
msgid "Hyphen (`-`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:41
msgid "Name"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:54
msgid ""
"Exactly how the digest is calculated depends on the type of store object "
"being referenced. Store path digests are _supposed_ to be opaque, and so for "
"most operations, it is not necessary to know the details. That said, the "
"manual has a full [specification of store path digests](../protocols/store-"
"path.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:58
msgid "Store Directory Path"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:60
msgid ""
"Every [Nix store](./index.md) has a store directory path. This is an "
"absolute, lexically canonical (not containing any `..`, `.`, or similar) "
"path which points to the directory where all store objects are to be found."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:65 source/store/store-path.md:98
msgid "**Note**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:67
msgid ""
"The store directory defaults to `/nix/store`, but is in principle arbitrary."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:69
msgid "Store Path"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:71
msgid ""
"A store path is the pair of a store directory path and a [store path base "
"name](#base-name). It is rendered to a file system path as the concatenation "
"of"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:74
msgid "[Store directory path](#store-directory-path) (typically `/nix/store`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:75
msgid "Path separator (`/`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:76
msgid "The [store path base name](#base-name)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:86
msgid ""
"When we have fixed a given store, or given store directory path (that all "
"the stores in use share), the abstract syntax for store paths and the "
"abstract syntax for store path base names coincide: the store directory path "
"is known from context, so only the other two fields vary from one store path "
"to the next."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:88
msgid "Exposing Store Objects in OS File Systems"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:90
msgid ""
"Not every store can be accessed through the file system. But if the store "
"has a file system representation, the following should be true:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:93
msgid ""
"The store directory path is canonical: no prefix of the path (i.e. path of "
"the first _n_ path segments) points to a symlink. In other words, the store "
"directory can be looked up from the store directory path without following "
"any symlinks. (This condition is a separate condition in addition to the "
"\"lexical canonicity\" described above, which is a property of just the path "
"itself. This (regular) \"canonicity\" is a property about the path and the "
"filesystem it navigates jointly.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:100
msgid ""
"The [`allow-symlinked-store`](../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-allow-"
"symlinked-store) setting can be used to relax this requirement."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:102
msgid "The store directory path in fact points to a directory."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:104
msgid ""
"The store directory contains, for every store object in the store, the [file "
"system object](./file-system-object.md) of that store object at the "
"(rendered) [store path base name](#base-name). The permissions and other "
"metadata for these files in the store directory is in the normal form "
"described in [Exposing in OS file systems](./file-system-object/os-file-"
"system.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:107
msgid ""
"The above properties mean that the following file accesses will work. "
"Suppose we have a store available on the file system per the above rules, "
"and `q06x3jll2yfzckz2bzqak089p43ixkkq-firefox-33.1` is the store path base "
"name of a store object in that store."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:110
msgid ""
"Suppose that the store directory (path) is `/foo/bar`. Then, `/foo/bar/"
"q06x3jll2yfzckz2bzqak089p43ixkkq-firefox-33.1` exists and is the file system "
"object of that store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:113
msgid ""
"Suppose that we don't know what the store directory path of the store is, "
"but we do have a capability `storeDir` to the store directory on the file "
"system. (This would be a \"file descriptor\" on Unix, or a \"file handle\" "
"on Windows.) Then (using the Unix notation for this):"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:116
msgid ""
"```\n"
"openat(storeDir, \"q06x3jll2yfzckz2bzqak089p43ixkkq-firefox-33.1\", "
"O_NOFOLLOW)\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:119
msgid ""
"will succeed (so long as the file system object is not a symlink), and the "
"yielded capability will point to the file system object of that store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:121
msgid ""
"(The behavior for symlinks is harder to specify because of limitations in "
"POSIX.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:123
msgid "Relocating store objects"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:125
msgid ""
"The inclusion of the store directory path in the full rendered store path "
"means that the full rendered store path is not just derived from the "
"referenced store object itself, but depends on the store that the store "
"object is in. (And actually, all of the currently-supported ways of "
"computing the digest of a store path also depend on the store directory "
"path, as described in the [specification of store path digests](../protocols/"
"store-path.md). So this is also true even just for store path base names, in "
"general.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:129
msgid ""
"It is therefore important to consider which store a given store object "
"belongs to: Files in the store object can contain store paths, and processes "
"may read these paths. Nix can only guarantee referential integrity if store "
"paths do not cross store boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:133
msgid "One can only copy store objects to a different store if"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:135
msgid "The source and target stores' directories match"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:137
msgid "or"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:139
msgid ""
"The store object in question has no references, that is, contains no store "
"paths"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:141
msgid ""
"One cannot copy a store object to a store with a different store directory. "
"Instead, it has to be rebuilt, together with all its dependencies. It is in "
"general not enough to replace the store directory string in file contents, "
"as this may render executables unusable by invalidating their internal "
"offsets or checksums."
msgstr ""
